1:# Precision: 16-bit-float compositing, deep decode, 10-bit exports (road map "precision", issue batch B94)
5:  - PR1, the 16-bit-float working depth and output, with its fix pass (`914b56a`; `word_looks_baseline.json` re-recorded on its Release build in `bb5de2a`)
6:  - PR2, deep decode (`8cb26d9`)
7:  - PR3, the `hevc_10bit` row, the documents, the close (uncommitted when this was written)
10:  - PR-1: the working depth is 16-bit float holding encoded values clamped to [0, 1]. The render context's buffers are `64RGBAHalf`; every target a layer pipeline draws into, and every layer, blend, shadow and dissolve pipeline's attachment, is `rgba16Float`; the coverage stays `r8Unorm` and the placeholder `bgra8Unorm`; the source sample and the blend and dissolve results are `saturate`d; blending stays in encoded sRGB; the preview shows the same buffers, no EDR or HDR. Amends MT-6, MT-8 and MT-9's figures. The measured memory (heavy 4K export +851.7 MB) was accepted by the owner.
13:  - PR-3: the export reader's frames are `64RGBAHalf`; `prores_422`, `prores_4444` and the new `hevc_10bit` row (Main 10, after `hevc_alpha`) take them as they are; `hevc` is pinned to Main; `h264` and `hevc` are rounded to 8-bit BGRA first; amends G-35 (c): `prores_4444` is un-premultiplied in float, `hevc_alpha` rounded to 8 bits and then straightened unchanged.
14:  - PR-4: `render_frame`, Save Frame…, GIF and APNG write the generator's CGImage, 8-bit when every source is 8-bit and 16-bit float when any is deeper (today's behaviour, written down).
15:  - PR-5: graphics, text, shapes, word captions, stills, thumbnails, the reversed-clip intermediate, screen recordings and the `r8` coverage stay 8-bit; a frozen reference compares decoded pixels within ±1, or prints its digest unscored.
19:The compositor worked in 8-bit BGRA end to end, so a 10-bit camera file lost its gradients on the way in and a ProRes export wrote 8-bit pictures into a 10-bit file. A scoping spike (`~/.claude/litecode/reelcut/precision/S-precision.md`, 2026-10-09) measured the costs and offered seven choices; the owner answered "As recommended" the same day: 16-bit float inside the compositor, every video source decoded deep, ProRes 10-bit automatically, a new HEVC 10-bit row, the HEVC row pinned to 8-bit Main, `render_frame`'s depth rule written down, the preview showing the same deep frames with no HDR, and graphics, text, stills, thumbnails, the reverse intermediate and recordings left 8-bit. HDR, wide gamut and linear light were deferred, and precision was ordered before the colour-grading branch. `Compositor.swift` (961 lines) paid its toll first: `LayerUniforms`, `ShadowUniforms` and `TransitionCode` moved whole to `LayerUniforms.swift` (orig PR0).
23:- **The 8-bit codecs are handed 8-bit frames.** Fed half-float frames, H.264 encoded the precision ramp at 8.80 steps RMS against 6.27 from 32BGRA, and `hevc` (Main) at 9.04 against 6.46. So `ExportWriter.frameForm` rounds `h264`'s and `hevc`'s frames to 8-bit BGRA (round(v·255)) in a pooled 32BGRA buffer (`ExportFrames`, `ExportStraightAlphaHalf.swift`), and their encoder input is what it was before the road map. `hevc` was added to this in PR1's fix pass. (orig PR1, "The review's fix pass")
24:- **The `hevc` pin is Main, and its mutant hands it deep frames.** Given no profile key, VideoToolbox follows its input's format, and deep frames gave Main 10. With 8-bit frames it writes Main with or without the pin, so a mutant that only removes the pin is equivalent; `hevc-no-profile` removes the pin AND hands `hevc` the deep frame. (orig PR1)
25:- **`hevc_10bit` is `hevc` by codec type and decided by codec NAME.** `codecType(for:)` answers `.hevc` for both HEVC rows, so `frameForm` and the profile ask `isMain10(settings)` (`settings.codec == "hevc_10bit"`) first; otherwise the new row would have been rounded to 8 bits. Mutant `hevc10-eightbit` removes that arm. (orig PR3)
26:- **An 8-bit input is caught by its staircase, not by its error.** Main 10 handed 8-bit frames encoded the ramp at 1.794 steps RMS against 1.459 from deep frames, inside both of T2's RMS bounds as first written, so the mutant survived. T2 now also reads the 8-bit staircase's tone (each column's mean code over the frame, less a 31-column moving average, at 255 steps across the width): 0.014 codes from deep frames, 0.536 from 8-bit ones and 0.758 for `hevc`, which is T2's control that the instrument sees a staircase at all. (orig PR3)
